WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.345
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Errors in
your field are unavoidable,

00:00:02.345 --> 00:00:04.470
and it's important to know
when they happen and get

00:00:04.470 --> 00:00:06.510
enough information
to fix them quickly.

00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:08.500
Writing out error
logs is easy enough,

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:10.985
but how do you send those
messages to Stackdriver?

00:00:10.985 --> 00:00:13.110
Let's look at how to get
your logs into Stackdriver

00:00:13.110 --> 00:00:14.550
and what you can do from there.

00:00:14.550 --> 00:00:15.870
This is the Stack Doctor.

00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:19.710
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:25.500
There are multiple ways to
log errors in Google Cloud.

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:28.500
You can use logging and set
your log severity to error

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:31.230
or you can use error reporting
to get powerful tracking

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:32.970
and analysis capabilities.

00:00:32.970 --> 00:00:34.755
Today, I want to show
you how both work

00:00:34.755 --> 00:00:36.880
and help you understand
how to choose between them.

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:38.970
Let's start with a simple
example of a web server

00:00:38.970 --> 00:00:40.620
written in node.js.

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:41.922
The app encounters an error.

00:00:41.922 --> 00:00:43.380
So something has
gone wrong, but we

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:45.030
don't have any other details.

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:47.360
You'll want to write those
details to an error log,

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:49.050
but there are a few
ways to do that.

00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:52.900
Your first option is to use the
Cloud Logging Library directly.

00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:55.770
However, it requires you to
explicitly build each log entry

00:00:55.770 --> 00:00:58.502
by manually specifying your
metadata before writing it.

00:00:58.502 --> 00:01:00.210
This can be useful if
you need this level

00:01:00.210 --> 00:01:02.380
of fine-grained control
over the data you're writing

00:01:02.380 --> 00:01:04.830
but could be tedious if
you're doing a lot of logging.

00:01:04.830 --> 00:01:07.230
More commonly, you may choose
to either use the Bunyan

00:01:07.230 --> 00:01:09.390
or Winston logging libraries.

00:01:09.390 --> 00:01:12.170
To use the Bunyan library,
simply add it to your code,

00:01:12.170 --> 00:01:13.950
create a new instance
of the logger,

00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:16.500
and then write error logs
using the .error method.

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:18.817
Using the Winston
library is very similar.

00:01:18.817 --> 00:01:21.150
Create a new instance of the
logger and write error logs

00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:22.650
using the .error method.

00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:24.600
In both cases,
structured logs will be

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:26.060
written to Stackdriver Logging.

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:28.560
The main difference between the
Bunyan and Winston libraries

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:30.810
is the verbosity of
the resulting logs.

00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:33.360
The Bunyan log messages
contain a lot more metadata,

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:35.118
potentially making
them more useful,

00:01:35.118 --> 00:01:37.410
but resulting in higher log
volumes and possibly higher

00:01:37.410 --> 00:01:38.142
costs.

00:01:38.142 --> 00:01:39.600
Consider both of
these factors when

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:41.020
choosing your logging approach.

00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:42.840
So now we have a
way to log basic app

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:44.065
errors on our node server.

00:01:44.065 --> 00:01:45.690
From here, we can do
things like create

00:01:45.690 --> 00:01:48.457
log base metrics to be notified
when these errors occur.

00:01:48.457 --> 00:01:50.790
But what happens if these
errors are actually exceptions

00:01:50.790 --> 00:01:52.125
that contain stack traces?

00:01:52.125 --> 00:01:54.000
If you log an exception,
the full stack trace

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:55.740
is available on the
log entry, making

00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:57.580
it easy to understand
exactly what happened.

00:01:57.580 --> 00:01:59.580
However, it's still hard
to get your information

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:01.020
across your exceptions.

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:03.270
This is where you can use
Stackdriver Error Reporting,

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:06.020
which automatically identifies
exceptions, groups them based

00:02:06.020 --> 00:02:08.580
on the stack trace, and
creates a histogram showing you

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:09.953
when these exceptions occur.

00:02:09.953 --> 00:02:11.370
There's no additional
setup needed

00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:14.250
to get error reporting to
identify errors or stack traces

00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:14.750
in them.

00:02:14.750 --> 00:02:16.458
You can look at the
details of each error

00:02:16.458 --> 00:02:19.170
to see the full stack trace and
the individual logger records

00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:20.430
that contain that error.

00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:22.170
You can then subscribe
to notifications

00:02:22.170 --> 00:02:24.420
to know when new errors are
detected in your project

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:26.040
and link errors to
bugs to keep track

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:27.625
of errors you fixed over time.

00:02:27.625 --> 00:02:30.000
Note that you can actually
use the error reporting client

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.200
to send errors directly
to error reporting,

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:33.900
even if they don't
have stack traces.

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:35.710
If you do that,
they're also written

00:02:35.710 --> 00:02:37.620
to Stackdriver Logging
along with other logs

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:40.410
you write, allowing you to reap
the benefits of both logging

00:02:40.410 --> 00:02:41.462
and error reporting.

00:02:41.462 --> 00:02:43.920
You have multiple options for
writing errors and exceptions

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:46.140
to Stackdriver Logging,
and using error reporting

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:48.390
can be a good way to get a
better understanding of how

00:02:48.390 --> 00:02:50.160
and when crashes
happen in your app

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:52.170
without giving up the
benefits of logging.

00:02:52.170 --> 00:02:54.760
Thank you for joining me today,
and come back soon for more.

00:02:54.760 --> 00:02:55.950
This is the Stack Doctor.

00:02:55.950 --> 00:02:56.940
Stay healthy out there.

00:02:56.940 --> 00:03:00.590
[MUSIC PLAYING]

